e and  share information so how can  this uh how can these kinds these types  of reference data be used  when diagnosing patients when analyzing  whole exomes  so there are different purposes of these  data types so first of all the known  variants you can use  to reduce the number of false negatives  they help you  avoid to miss a variant to miss a  variant that has been previously  reported and  in the same way the phenotype  information works  it helps you to prioritize to rank  the genes and to the degree that they  overlap with the known phenotype of the  patient  so they help you to reduce numbers or  false positive false negatives  and the then on the other hand the  reference data from healthy populations  work  exactly the opposite opposite way they  help you to  filter out all variants that just appear  too frequently  across healthy populations and therefore  the simple argument is  what is occurring to too frequently  cannot be pathogenic this is a thinking  that  is valid in this setting of dealing with  um single gene diseases and  where the explanation is a single  variant  and last but not least the real world  evidence gives you both  it gives you the ability to reduce  reduce false positives  like the healthy reference populations  because every patient that is contained  in this real world evidence database  can serve as a control for the patient  that you currently look at  because pathogenic variants are just so  rare  and the reward evidence can also serve  to reduce the number of false negatives  of course  because you have access to additional  evidence  to be able to classify variance as  pathogenic  i'd like to explain a little bit more  what the quality of the data  is that is contained in real world  evidence databases  if you compare or if you're looking in  more detail at nomad you know  it's gnomid is a database it has been  built  from data obtained from healthy  adults it has been  curated very diligently but still  you may know that