lt to deal  with this information and to really make  use of the data that is contained in  clinvar  so now that i've talked a lot about the  theory of  data sources and how you can utilize  them in the  whole exome diagnostic process  i would like to explain how a good  solution a validated solution  can look like and this is our software  solution varvis  so varvis covers basically and supports  the workflow  starting from the raw data from the  sequencing instruments it covers  the three steps on the right the  bioinformatics the filtering and the  reporting  but most importantly in the very  beginning it gives you immediate  feedback  about the quality of your data and this  is something that  we work with our customers very closely  is monitoring this the laboratory  process and helping them  to optimize and to achieve the level of  quality that's required for  routine diagnostics the software  presents to you  a very clean interface  to collect all this data all the data is  going and stored  into a database so you have the ability  to monitor data over time  and you have the ability you can set  thresholds  for quality parameters and you will be  notified  if any of your samples fails any of  those thresholds here  so you have a very quick overview if  everything is going to plan  and you also know very very quickly  if you have to optimize into a certain  direction  of course a core component of varvis  is the bioinformatics component and  since  varvis is a cloud native  decision support software based and  operated in the cloud  it is very easy to set up and you have  no investment to make into it  infrastructure  that means you also can scale up very  quickly from  small panels to whole genomes or from a  small number of samples to a very large  number of samples  but what is very important from our  point of view is  that the processes are validated and  they are validated both in the sv  analysis and the cnv analysis and we  actually provide this validation  as a service to our customers and this  is how  this looks like in practice you  our customers simply sequence specific  reference samples  as per our instructions and those  samples  go through the regular process as any  other sample  in your laboratory and we  then compare the results in an automated  fashion  with the reference data that means you  obtain  automated validation reports every time  you sequence such a sample  and we also repeat this validation every  time  something changes in the software so you  can always be sure  that the results that you obtain from  the software are valid  the same level of diligence will also  apply to the cnb analysis  we have performed validation projects on  thousands of samples obtained from our  customers  and we always compare to the gold  standard and cnb  analysis which are pcr based methods  and we obtain 100 sensitivity  if the samples comply with our quality  criteria  and at the same time we achieve a very  high specificity  and this is very important if you want  to apply this technology  as a screening tool as first line  diagnostics  if you have a very high false positive  rate here  this will be this would be very cost  inefficient  because you would have to perform a lot  of additional testing  to confirm the many variants that you  find here  so in practice our customers  have all samples being analyzed for cnvs  there's a specific quality control  because the cnv analysis is  more sensitive to quality than the snv  analysis  but in the end most of of the samples  are undergoing this process  and since we achieve such a high  sensitivity  you can be sure if you don't find a cnv  in the data you can be sure that there  is none  and you can very quickly write a  negative report  so then the other step in the process  that we  pay a lot of attention to is the  filtering and the classification  and i explained before what  uh the real world evidence is and what  it is made up  of and we enable you to capture  this evidence and capture the entire  picture of the data of the patient  so it starts from data about how the  data  was generated how the wet lab process  was performed  information from the sequencing  instrument information from  the bioinformatics processing and you  can  amend this information with family  relations with the entire family history  and of course you can add the phenotype  the classification of variance  and the final diagnosis so you bring  everything together  that you have about a patient and this  is stored in a centralized database  and this then enables you to actually  systematically  manage the data that you are generating  so in the beginning we enable you to  capture the data from the single patient  using standardized ontologies but then  you are collecting with every other  patient that you're  diagnosing you're building your own  local database  and this database typically consists of  homogeneous data sets  and you have full access to the analysis  history  and on top of that we aggregate data  from  many different institutions and create a  reference data network  and this allows you to have access  to many data sets in compliance with  international regulations  and i'd like to explain in detail how  this works  and why we are complying with  international regulations  the protection of patient data is  crucial in this setup and is implemented  into the software by design  so if you think of two laboratories  laboratory x and y  or seeing different patients who are  performing diagnosis on different  patients  they transmit only de-identified data to  a centralized database  we're only interested in phenotype  information and genotype information  and if you now think of yourself or your  laboratory  performing whole exome sequencing on a  patient and this is maybe you're the  first data set that you're seeing  you receive annotations to every single  variant  in your data set from the centralized  reference data set  so you receive information how often has  this variant been found in other  patients  has this variant ever been classified in  other patients  and if it has been found in other  patients what are the phenotype  descriptions what are the hbo terms that  are associated to those patients  but it will never be possible to you to  actually access  the entire data set of a different  patient this is entirely protected  now some facts and figures what is  actually not  contained in the database this is data  from  march of this year we have information  in the database  of more than 19 million unique small  variants  the data has been generated from 35 000  patients  those patients are part of 21 000  different families  and 17 000 of those patients have been  described diligently  using standardized ontologies which  makes the data very useful  in the analysis of whole exome data sets  we also collect information of copy  number variations  so at this moment more than 100 000  copy number variations are contained in  the database  and this is data that was generated from  more than 6000 different cases  so this is already a very powerful and  very comprehensive  collection of data sets and now i'd like  to  use a few examples to highlight  the utility and practice of those data  sets  so my first example will just will show  in a very simple  manner how utilizing the alexis database  can accelerate your search for  significant variants  so in this example this is a diseased  child the parents are healthy  and the entire trio was sequenced using  whole exome sequencing  we entered the phenotype into the  software and we are also entering  the family relations between the  individuals into the database so  all three data sets are linked and can  be analyzed accordingly  this is being utilized this information  is being utilized by the software  in the analysis so let's go  through the data review step by step we  are basically starting with  more than 40 000 variants that have been  found  in the patient i'm now applying  very simple filtering against the nomad  database  this is a very severe phenotype and i'm  filtering  only for very rare data variants in the  nomad database so this reduces  the set that i'm looking at to a little  more than 1000 variants  if i additionally apply a filter against  the alexis database  i can even further reduce this number by  a factor of two  so it takes away fifty percent of the  variance that  i would have otherwise have further  downstream have to look at  so this is a very powerful filtering and  it works in the same way as if you  already had  sequenced uh tens of thousands of whole  exomes yourself  and the filtering works um very easily  in the software  you can store any filter configuration  for your data sets in buttons and you  can see here in the screenshot  the yellow buttons are the activated  filters and well just with the press of  two buttons i  can get to the stage of filtering i now  apply  further filtering for impact of those  variants  with respect to any known transcript of  the genes that are they are related to  and i can also apply a  filtering or rather a ranking of my  variants  with respect to how well the phenotype  of the patient  matches with known phenotype  descriptions of the genes  so i end up with about 30 variants in  the end  that i would have to look at in more  detail but  i haven't made use of the family  relations yet so i'm now  filtering for different hypotheses  what the inheritance pattern would be  in my in my trio data set  and i then end up with just three  variants that would fulfill an autosomal  dominant  inheritance patterns and three variants  from  autosomal recessive compound  heterozygous inheritance pattern  so in the end i only have to look at six  variants in detail  and this is a manageable number so it's  always  the goal of the reviewer to reduce the  number of variants to look at  below 10. anything be above that would  be  too much to handle and too much work in  a root routine diagnostic  process  so this is a screenshot of the result in  the software you can see that  the filters that i mentioned were  applied  these are in the screen shot in the back  you can see three three variants that  are  left in the autosomal recessive  component heterozygous filter  and one of those variants is selected  it's  the result is that two of those  heterozygous variants  are identified as causative for the  phenotype a compound heterozygous state  and you can also see that these variants  are now known in the lexus database and  you  wouldn't even miss them because you can  now filter also for pathogenic variants  in those genes my second example  highlights how important it is to  perform  cnv analysis on all of your samples  the cnv data from whole exome sequencing  is so rich  it can basically replace area  diagnostics  and this is an example where two de novo  cnvs were found and this is so  simple that it can be seen at a glance  from those data sets  this is a screenshot from the  exam trio so you can see at the top  the index patient the the child and  the bottom the father and the mother and  there are two  variants visible one variant and both  variants are de novo because  they are not visible in the data sets of  the parents so there's one deletion on  chromosome 21  and nearby there's also a duplication so  this was  again a very clear case and very easily  visible  and highlights very nicely how much  the cnv analysis complements the snv  analysis and the clinical diagnostic  so these this is a slide just  highlighting what you can do  if you have access to an optimized  software platform in using whole exome  diagnostics  there's an example of a laboratory that  started working with us  in 2016. in 2016 they  didn't do any significant number of  whole exomes yet  and as but as you can see by utilizing  uh the power of the barbary solution  they were able to  basically double the number of samples  that they are processing every year  and from the numbers of the just of the  first quarter  they are going to process a few thousand  samples  and this year and i have to point out  that they don't have a bioinformatician  that is involved in the clinical  diagnostics process  and they have published as you can see  you can see here in this link  that a trained scientist can easily  handle 700 cases per year  using this kind of setup  so this concludes my presentation  how diagnostic process  for whole exome sequencing should be set  up  we are free very frequently discussing  topics  around whole exome sequencing in our  blog  feel free to check it out and to provide  us with feedback if there are some  topics that we haven't touched so far  maybe you're also interested in  workshops about clinical diagnostics  using whole exome sequencing  we are hosting these kinds of workshops  and the workshops are conducted by  clinical experts  with a lot of experience with whole  exome sequencing  if you're interested get in touch with  us at events  varvers.com thank you
 

